Name: sessionCookieSameSite

Type: property

Syntax: set the sessionCookieSameSite to {empty | "lax" | "strict" | "none"}

Syntax: get the sessionCookieSameSite

Summary:
Specifies the SameSite attribute of the cookie that stores the session
id, which says whether browsers return it with requests that come from
other sites.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: server

Example:
set the sessionCookieSameSite to "lax"
start session

Example:
-- a session used from pages of another site (HTTPS only)
set the sessionCookieSecure to true
set the sessionCookieSameSite to "none"
start session

Value (enum):
The SameSite attribute of the session cookie. By default, it is empty.

-   empty: the cookie has no SameSite attribute (the default), and the
    browser applies its own rule, which in current browsers is that of
    "lax"
-   "lax": the cookie is not sent with requests another site makes,
    such as its images, frames and form posts, but is sent when the
    user follows a link to the site
-   "strict": the cookie is sent only with requests that come from the
    site's own pages
-   "none": the cookie is sent with every request, whichever site makes
    it. Browsers accept this only for a cookie that is also Secure, so
    set the <sessionCookieSecure> to true as well

Description:
Use the <sessionCookieSameSite> property to say how the session cookie
is treated in requests from other sites, as a protection against
cross-site request forgery, where a page of another site makes the
user's browser send a request with the user's session.

When a session is started, a cookie is created in the browser to
identify the ongoing session. By default that cookie has no SameSite
attribute, as it never had. Setting the <sessionCookieSameSite> adds
the attribute with the value given. Setting it to anything but one of
the four values is an error.

Set the <sessionCookieSameSite> before the <start session> command (and
before <delete session>, whose expired cookie takes the same
attributes). It is not saved anywhere: a site that wants it sets it on
every request, for example in a file every page includes.

>*Note:* You do not need to alter any of the session properties in order
> to start and use sessions. They provide a way for advanced users to
> configure the way session work.

>*Note:* The sessionCookieSameSite property can be read and set in
> every engine, and has an effect only when running in CGI mode
> (Server).

References: sessionCookieSecure (property),
sessionCookieName (property), sessionSavePath (property),
sessionLifetime (property), sessionID (property),
start session (command), delete session (command), put cookie (command)
